**Proposition 8.2 (an explicit two-copy matching law).** Set
$m=2,\ell=2,\epsilon=1/4$. Label the four phase-zero solutions by
their two edge choices, in the order $aa,ab,ba,bb$, and place the
phase-one solution last. The single-gadget Gibbs probabilities are

$$
p=\frac1{1250}(81,144,144,256,625).
$$

Writing $f(s)$ for a row's phase, the two-copy laws on all $25$ actual
rows are exactly

$$
P(s,t)=p(s)p(t),\qquad
Q(s,t)=
\begin{cases}
\frac74p(s)p(t),&f(s)=f(t),\\
\frac14p(s)p(t),&f(s)\ne f(t).
\end{cases}
$$

Their phase law under $Q$, in order $00,01,10,11$, is

$$
q=(7/16,1/16,1/16,7/16).
\tag{OCC.28}
$$

Both laws are normalized, have identical entire-gadget marginals and
agree on every full conditional update of at most seven actual owners.
Their entropy and free-energy difference is exactly $2-H(q)>0$.

Proof. In phase zero the two independent choices have probabilities
$9/25,16/25$ by Theorem 5.2. Multiplying their products by the phase
mass $1/2$ gives the first four entries of $p$; the last entry is
$1/2$. In (OCC.22), $2^\ell q$ equals $7/4$ on equal phases and
$1/4$ on unequal phases, proving the stated $Q$. Each gadget has
phase mass $1/2$, so summing either conditional multiplier over the
other gadget gives $(7/4+1/4)/2=1$. This proves normalization and
both entire-gadget marginals directly. An update of at most seven
owners cannot change a phase because its crossing distance is eight.
The multiplier $Q/P$ therefore cancels on each such conditional
fiber. Finally apply (OCC.21); the displayed $q$ is not uniform.
$\square$

